You're probably thinking 'Donaudampfschifffahrtskapit�nswitwe' and
similar large (or even larger) German words which are completely
natural to a native speaker.

But you have to see the difference. German is able to string arbitrary
nouns together. But there are agglutinative languages which glue
almost everything together when building sentences.

So constructing large words is not only natural to speakers of those
languages but it is even necessary and therefore the number of "words"
is almost infinite.

